    <h2 itemprop="headline">CGPA to Percentage — The Complete Guide for Indian Students</h2>
    <p>Whether you are filling out a job application, applying for higher studies, or simply trying to understand where you stand academically, knowing how to <strong>convert CGPA to percentage</strong> is an essential skill for every Indian university student. With the shift from traditional marking systems to credit-based grading, most universities now award a Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) instead of a raw percentage — yet most recruiters, government forms, and international universities still ask for a percentage equivalent.</p>

    <h2>What is CGPA and Why Does It Need Conversion?</h2>
    <p>CGPA stands for <strong>Cumulative Grade Point Average</strong>. It is a standardised measure of a student's overall academic performance across all semesters, calculated on a scale that most Indian universities define as 0 to 10. Each subject is assigned grade points based on the marks obtained, and these grade points are averaged — weighted by credit hours — to produce a single cumulative figure.</p>
    <p>The need for <strong>CGPA to percentage conversion</strong> arises because India's employment and education ecosystem has not fully transitioned to GPA-based evaluation. Central and state government jobs, PSUs, competitive examinations like GATE, and many multinational companies still define eligibility using a percentage threshold.</p>

    <h2>How to Convert CGPA to Percentage — The Standard Formula</h2>
    <p>The most widely accepted formula in India is the one prescribed by the Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE):</p>

    <div class="formula-block">
      <div class="formula-label">Standard CBSE Formula</div>
      <div class="formula-text">Percentage = CGPA × 9.5</div>
      <div class="formula-example">Example: 8.4 CGPA × 9.5 = <strong>79.8%</strong></div>
    </div>

    <p>This multiplier of 9.5 is derived from the historical observation that the average student scoring a grade point of 10 typically obtains around 95 marks out of 100. When you use a <strong>CGPA to percentage calculator</strong> online, it almost always defaults to this multiplier unless you specify otherwise.</p>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Calculation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Locate your CGPA on your grade card or marksheet — it is usually printed as a decimal number between 0.00 and 10.00.</li>
      <li>Identify your university's official formula (see the table above for 8 universities).</li>
      <li>Multiply your CGPA by the appropriate factor to get your percentage.</li>
      <li>If your university uses a different multiplier, apply that formula instead.</li>
      <li>For official documents, always obtain a percentage equivalence certificate from your university registrar.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>CGPA to Percentage Conversion for SPPU (Savitribai Phule Pune University)</h2>
    <p>Students affiliated with <strong>Savitribai Phule Pune University (SPPU)</strong> — one of the largest universities in Asia by enrolled students — follow a distinct grading ordinance that does not use the standard 9.5 multiplier. The official <strong>SPPU CGPA to percentage</strong> formula is:</p>

    <div class="formula-block">
      <div class="formula-label">SPPU Formula (Engineering – 10-point scale)</div>
      <div class="formula-text">Percentage = (CGPA × 10) − 7.5</div>
      <div class="formula-example">Example: 8.5 CGPA → (8.5 × 10) − 7.5 = <strong>77.5%</strong></div>
    </div>

    <h3>When Does the SPPU Formula Apply?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>B.E. / B.Tech programmes under SPPU on the credit-based grading system (CBGS).</li>
      <li>Postgraduate engineering programmes (M.E. / M.Tech) affiliated to SPPU.</li>
      <li>Any course where your grade card explicitly states a 10-point grading scale under SPPU affiliation.</li>
      <li>For non-engineering faculties (Arts, Commerce, Science), consult your department — SPPU may apply a separate conversion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Using a CGPA to Percentage Converter — What to Look For</h2>
    <p>A reliable <strong>CGPA to percentage converter</strong> should do more than blindly multiply by 9.5:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It offers a university selector so you can apply the correct formula for your institution.</li>
      <li>It handles edge cases — a CGPA above 10 should be capped, not silently output a wrong number.</li>
      <li>It is transparent about which formula it is using, displaying it alongside the result.</li>
      <li>It works offline and does not require you to create an account or submit data to a server.</li>
      <li>It provides a reverse converter (percentage to CGPA) for situations where you need to verify eligibility.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <strong>CGPA to percentage calculator</strong> on this page satisfies all of the above. It runs entirely in your browser with zero data collection, supports 8 universities, and shows you the exact formula alongside every result.</p>
